The '''2024 [[Madrid Open (tennis)|Mutua Madrid Open]]''' was a professional [[tennis]] tournament played on outdoor [[clay court]]s at the [[Caja Mágica|Park Manzanares]] in [[Madrid]], Spain from 23 April to 5 May 2024. It was the 22nd edition of the event on the [[ATP Tour]] and 15th on the [[WTA Tour]]. It was classified as an [[ATP Tour Masters 1000]] event on the [[2024 ATP Tour]] and a [[WTA 1000 tournaments|WTA 1000]] event on the [[2024 WTA Tour]]. The Tournament paid tribute to the five time and most successful champion in the men's side Rafael Nadal, on his last appearance at the tournament.<ref>{{cite web|title=Mutua Madrid Open Overview|url=https://www.atptour.com/en/tournaments/madrid/1536/overview|website=atptour.com}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Mutua Madrid Open Overview|url=https://www.wtatennis.com/tournament/1038/madrid|website=wtatennis.com}}</ref> 

== Champions ==

=== Men's singles ===
{{main|2024 Mutua Madrid Open – Men's singles}}
* {{flagicon|}} '''[[Andrey Rublev]]''' def. {{flagicon|CAN}} [[Félix Auger-Aliassime]], 4–6, 7–5, 7–5

=== Women's singles ===
{{main|2024 Mutua Madrid Open – Women's singles}}
* {{flagicon|POL}} '''[[Iga Świątek]]''' def. {{flagicon|}} [[Aryna Sabalenka]], 7–5, 4–6, 7–6<sup>(9–7)</sup>

This was Świątek's third title of the year, and 20th of her career.

=== Men's doubles ===
{{main|2024 Mutua Madrid Open – Men's doubles}}
* {{flagicon|USA}} '''[[Sebastian Korda]]''' / {{flagicon|AUS}} '''[[Jordan Thompson (tennis)|Jordan Thompson]]''' def. {{flagicon|URU}} [[Ariel Behar]] / {{flagicon|CZE}} [[Adam Pavlásek]], 6–3, 7–6<sup>(9–7)</sup>

=== Women's doubles ===
{{main|2024 Mutua Madrid Open – Women's doubles}}
* {{flagicon|ESP}} '''[[Cristina Bucșa]]''' / {{flagicon|ESP}} '''[[Sara Sorribes Tormo]]''' def. {{flagicon|CZE}} [[Barbora Krejčíková]] / {{flagicon|GER}} [[Laura Siegemund]] 6–0, 6–2

